[test-suite name=net
  [doc [#
conformance fixtures for cx-stdlib/net (spec/02-inprogress/stdlib_net.md §10).

   Each program imports the bundled module via [?lib 'cx-stdlib/net']
   (resolver MUST be quoted) then calls [$net:fn args]. The LAST
   expression is the rendered result.

   DETERMINISM / EFFECTFUL MODULE: cx-stdlib/net is a Tier-B, necessarily
   impure module. Every SOCKET/RESOLVER function is `impure` (§3) and every
   one of its effect points is gated on the `net` capability (§5). The
   stdlib eval runner evaluates each case under `code.new_env()`, which
   grants NO capabilities (deny-by-default per spec/core/security.md §2). At
   the effect point the active set is checked FIRST; with the grant absent
   the function raises E_CAP_DENIED = `cx-err:CXER0271` (security.md §4).

   Consequently the deterministic, runner-faithful, spec-grounded assertion
   for the happy-path call of every GATED net function is the capability-
   denied error CXER0271 — real socket I/O (connect/bind/accept) is neither
   reproducible nor safe inside the conformance harness, and AUTHORING.md
   ("Capability lanes") directs us to fixture the capability-denied error
   rather than real effects. Each gated function therefore gets a CXER0271
   case exercising its real call shape (correct arity / arg kinds per the
   §3 [?def] signature). The capability-GRANTED behavior (real round-trips,
   TLS handshake, accept-iter, SSRF deny) belongs in a capability-granted
   harness (implementation-phase task) and is gate=pending here.

   CAPABILITY-FREE FUNCTIONS (§5): parse-addr and addr-to-string are PURE
   (no `net` grant, real output even under the empty set); close / is-open /
   local-addr / remote-addr inspect already-known local handle state (no
   I/O), so they too run capability-free. These get real-output cases.

   NET ERROR CODES (CXER4500–CXER4524, §8): net owns CXER4500–4524. The
   rev-7 draft's CXER44xx band collided with cx-stdlib/fp (CXER4400–4409,
   governance §9.6); net was reallocated the next free 25-code block. The
   restricted-transport-URL errors (CXER4500 E_NET_ADDR_INVALID, CXER4501
   E_NET_SCHEME_UNSUPPORTED) fire inside the PURE parse, ahead of any cap
   check, so they are asserted directly. The connection/TLS codes
   (CXER4502+) fire only AFTER the cap check passes, so under the empty set
   a correct impl raises CXER0271 — NOT the net-specific code — for those
   scenarios; they belong in the granted harness (gate=pending).
#]]

 [; §3.1 resolution & addresses — parse-addr / addr-to-string PURE ]

 [case id=net-001-parse-addr-tcp level=core
  [tags net parse-addr address pure]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/net']
[$net:parse-addr 'tcp://example.com:443']
#]]
  [out-text [#
[addr host='example.com' port=443 family=ipv4]
#]]
 ]

 [case id=net-002-parse-addr-tls level=core
  [tags net parse-addr tls pure]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/net']
[$net:parse-addr 'tls://example.com:443']
#]]
  [out-text [#
[addr host='example.com' port=443 family=ipv4]
#]]
 ]

